From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 23:55:37 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] u_thread: leave the synchronous fault signals unblocked on
 driver threads

u_thread_create() blocks every signal but SIGSYS on the calling thread around
thrd_create(), so the thread it creates - and every thread that thread creates
in turn - inherits SIGSEGV, SIGBUS, SIGILL and SIGFPE blocked.

On Darwin a synchronous fault signal that is blocked on the thread which takes
the fault is never delivered. The thread returns to the faulting instruction and
takes the same fault again, forever, with no handler call and no report: a
process that should have died with a diagnosable crash instead burns a whole
core inside the kernel, and the only way to find out why is to sample it. That
was measured on an Apple host: a worker thread with SIGSEGV blocked spins on the
faulting instruction, while the same thread with SIGSEGV unblocked reports the
fault and exits.

The threads this helper creates - the vrend sync thread and a DRM fence
timeline's - are ones that run renderer work and can fault like any other, so
they must be able to report it. Mesa's own src/util/u_thread.c already spares
SIGSEGV here for a related reason; spare the rest of the synchronous fault
signals as well, in both copies' spirit: a thread may block what it likes, but
not the signals that describe its own faults.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/mesa/util/u_thread.h | 12 ++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/mesa/util/u_thread.h b/src/mesa/util/u_thread.h
index 366c8e9217eec736c8fb0bd377c8d58270916369..933a3595c4cd93ccefbdc89b6433b845f641840c 100644
--- a/src/mesa/util/u_thread.h
+++ b/src/mesa/util/u_thread.h
@@ -113,6 +113,18 @@ static inline thrd_t u_thread_create(int (*routine)(void *), void *param)
 
    sigfillset(&new_set);
    sigdelset(&new_set, SIGSYS);
+   /* A synchronous fault signal that is blocked on the thread which takes the
+    * fault is never delivered: the thread returns to the faulting instruction
+    * and retries it forever, with no handler call and no report. The mask set
+    * here is inherited by the thread being created and by every thread it
+    * creates in turn, so blocking these would turn any fault on a driver
+    * thread into a silent spin. Mesa's own copy of this helper leaves SIGSEGV
+    * unblocked for the same reason.
+    */
+   sigdelset(&new_set, SIGSEGV);
+   sigdelset(&new_set, SIGBUS);
+   sigdelset(&new_set, SIGILL);
+   sigdelset(&new_set, SIGFPE);
    pthread_sigmask(SIG_BLOCK, &new_set, &saved_set);
    ret = thrd_create( &thread, routine, param );
    pthread_sigmask(SIG_SETMASK, &saved_set, NULL);
